# Cap the NVMe/TCP command size on fabric namespaces.
#
# WHY. The TrueNAS target advertises MDTS = 0 -- no limit -- so the command size
# is decided entirely on the host: 1280 KB by default, and up to 16 MB on newer
# kernels. nvmet_tcp on TrueNAS 25.10.x fails to map data buffers for very large
# read commands and returns Internal Error, which surfaces as I/O errors during
# vzdump and disk moves (upstream #45, and most likely the window in which the
# corruption of #32 happened).
#
# WHY subsysnqn AND NOT transport. The obvious rule,
#
#     ATTRS{transport}=="tcp"
#
# never fires. With native multipath (nvme_core.multipath=Y) the transport
# attribute lives on the per-path controllers (nvme0c0n1), not on the namespace
# head (nvme0n1) that carries the queue limits. The head's parent exposes only
# subsysnqn and model. A rule written that way installs cleanly, reports no
# error, and does nothing at all -- verify, do not assume.
#
# WHY NOT a blanket nvme match. That would also throttle the node's local NVMe,
# which is where local-lvm lives. The NQN match is what keeps this off it.
#
# MEASURED (lab, 3-node cluster, kernel 6.8 and 7.0.2-pve):
#
#     head 1280 / paths 1280  ->  actual request size 1024 KB
#     head  128 / paths 1280  ->  actual request size  128 KB
#     head 1024 / paths 1280  ->  actual request size 1024 KB     <- this rule (idk22)
#     head  128 / paths  128  ->  actual request size  128 KB
#
# Setting it on the head is enough: it splits the bio before forwarding to the
# path. Also verified to survive a full disconnect/reconnect of the subsystem,
# which is what matters -- namespaces come and go.
#
# VALUE. 1024 is the fork's in-plugin default (NVME_DEFAULT_MAX_IO_KB, tn_nvme_max_io_kb):
# the plugin re-applies that number on every revalidation, so a rule with a
# different one would make each pass report a drift it caused itself. Change both
# together; t/nvme/31-udev-cap-single-source.t compares them. Upstream's own
# measurement used 128, which also works but is not what this fork sets.
#
# INSTALL
#     The .deb installs this file as /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/99-nvme-tcp-max-sectors.rules;
#     nothing to copy. Do NOT also copy it to /etc/udev/rules.d/ under the same
#     name: udev lets that copy replace the packaged one, so upgrades would stop
#     updating the rule. (Without the package, install it by hand to
#     /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/ instead.)
#     udevadm control --reload-rules
#     udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=block --action=change
#     cat /sys/block/<dev>/queue/max_sectors_kb        # must say 1024
#
# COEXISTENCE. Production nodes may already carry
# /etc/udev/rules.d/99-nvmeof-maxio.rules (a different file name, which sets the
# same 1024 on the head with a RUN step). Both rules may stay: they write the
# same value, so neither undoes the other. Keep every one of them at the plugin's
# default (NVME_DEFAULT_MAX_IO_KB = tn_nvme_max_io_kb); a different number in any
# of them would make the plugin report a drift each time it revalidates.
#
# The trigger applies it to already-connected namespaces, so no reboot and no
# disconnect is needed. Confirm the value actually changed -- a rule that does
# not match fails silently.
#
# ROLLBACK
#     rm /etc/udev/rules.d/99-nvme-tcp-max-sectors.rules
#     udevadm control --reload-rules
#     echo 1280 > /sys/block/<dev>/queue/max_sectors_kb
#
# Adjust the NQN glob if the array's subsystem NQNs do not contain "truenas".

ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="nvme*n*", \
  ATTRS{subsysnqn}=="nqn.*truenas*", ATTR{queue/max_sectors_kb}="1024"
